Env         : resnet_env17
Branch      : worktree-ignition-dev
Produced by : a script calling quant.shift_cut.analyze_model_shift_cut directly over
              the eleven models, NOT eleven CLI invocations. Section 6 is the CLI's
              own output for one model, verbatim; the equivalent command is
              `python -m quant check-shift-cut --branches <model>`.
Hardware    : NONE. Static ONNX inspection only; no InferenceSession was built
              and no hardware context was opened. Safe under peer NPU load.
Host load   : OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 for every command in this sitting. Note that this
              does NOT make tools/host_load.ps1 report CLEAR -- `-m\s+quant\b` is
              a PEER_CMDS token there, so any `python -m quant` is PEER by name
              whatever its thread count, and CORES_PEER is 2.0 < 4 in any case.
              Nothing here is a producer run; the heaviest step is onnx.load.

This log supersedes nothing in results/quant/shift_cut_reaudit_20260909_desktop2.log.
Every violation count there still reads zero here. What it adds is why that zero was
never evidence, and what the analyzer was counting to reach it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. sigma and the vendor's shift_cut differ by a constant
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Quark's adjust_shift_cut (transcribed at quant/refine.py::shift_cut, sourced in
results/quant/notes_xint8_dialect.log:37 and :1216-1253) defines, for Conv and Gemm

    shift_cut = wpos + ipos - opos      clamped into [0, 16]

quant/shift_cut.py's Theorem 1 quantity over the same three positions is

    sigma = pos_x + pos_w - pos_y + 14

so  sigma == shift_cut + 14, and the producer's contract is exactly

    sigma in [14, 30]   <-  a strict subset of Theorem 1's [0, 31]

CONSEQUENCE. A Conv or Gemm in a file Quark or Ignition emitted cannot reach the
[0, 31] edges without the producer's own clamp having failed first. The "0 violations
on 11 of 11 models" result is therefore a tautology of the producer's clamp, not a
measurement of the hardware bound. The test is kept -- a hand-edited, CLE-inflated or
foreign-producer file is not in-contract -- but a PASS from it means "this file came
from the producer", never "this file is safe on the DPU".

This also explains, without new hardware, the fact recorded in 311a672 that the
highest sigma ever executed on this device is 30: 30 is CONTRACT_SIGMA's upper edge
(shift_cut = 16), i.e. the producer's ceiling, not the silicon's. The 5-bit shifter
and 15-bit multiplier remain unmeasured, exactly as that commit says.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Operations scored on scales that were never read
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
analyze_model_shift_cut initialised scale_x/scale_w/scale_y to 1.0 and overwrote each
only where a DequantizeLinear producer with an initializer scale existed. Where none
existed the operation was still scored, still got a sigma, and still counted as a
passing operation in the denominator.

Those rows are the sub-14 minima in the published distributions. They are the
`<out>_Scale` / `<out>_Mul` pairs that quant/passes.py::_insert_mul writes for DPU
simulation, whose two inputs are a Constant and a HardSigmoid -- not a QDQ pair.

They are now classified UNRESOLVED: excluded from the violation denominator and from
every distribution, and counted on their own line.

