Violations against [0, 31]: 0 on every model, matching the re-audit exactly.
Outside the producer contract: 0 on every model -- all 744 analyzed Conv/Gemm across
the eleven sit in [14, 30], which is what section 1 says must happen and
is now checked rather than assumed.

SCOPE, because getting this wrong would be the same defect again. The contract is
asserted of Conv and Gemm ONLY. adjust_shift_cut skips every other op_type, so the
18 analyzed MatMuls and 174 analyzed Muls get a sigma and the [0, 31] hazard test but
NO contract verdict -- they all happen to land inside [14, 30] here, and that is a
coincidence of these models rather than a rule. A Mul is refined by shift_write_mul
(clamp 0..32) and shift_swish (clamp 0..15), different rules over different
quantities; judging one against [14, 30] would report "this file was not emitted by
Quark/Ignition" about a file that was.

Coverage: 166 of 1102 candidate operations across the eleven models were
being scored on invented scales. The two head-cut YOLO files are the worst affected
(57 of 177 and 63 of 198), because SiLU leaves one _Scale/_Mul pair per activation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Contract-edge saturation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
An operation at sigma 14 or 30 is one the producer's clamp had hard against an edge.
Interior layers are the normal case; a model pinned at BOTH edges is one whose
per-channel ranges the producer could not fit.

model                          Conv/Gemm  at sigma=14  at sigma=30  both edges
resnet50_xint8_c64                    54            0            0       0.0%
yolov8n_cut_xint8                     63            0            0       0.0%
yolov8n-pose_cut_xint8                72            0            0       0.0%
regnetx_002_xint8                     45            4            2      13.3%  <-- TWO-SIDED
resnext50_32x4d_xint8                 54            4            4      14.8%  <-- TWO-SIDED
bisenetv2_fp32_xint8                  57            0            0       0.0%
fastdepth_fp32_xint8                  38            0            0       0.0%
sesr_m7_xint8                          9            0            0       0.0%
midas_small_cut_xint8                 97            0            0       0.0%
mobilevit_xint8                       72            0            0       0.0%
densenet121_xint8                    183           18            0       9.8%

Two models saturate at both edges: regnetx_002 and resnext50_32x4d. Those are exactly
the two the 311a672 commit identified as cross-layer-equalization confounds -- 0.10%
top-1 each, recovering to 66.20% and 68.90% with CLE off
(results/quant/eval_regnetx_002_quark_cle_full1000_cpu.log,
 results/quant/eval_regnetx_002_ignition_nocle_c64_cpu.log,
 results/quant/eval_resnext50_32x4d_quark_nocle_c64_cpu.log).

ATTRIBUTION, STATED HONESTLY. This is a correlation at n = 2, and it is NOT
established that equalization is the cause of the saturation.

  - The alternative not ruled out is architecture. Both saturating models are
    grouped-convolution designs (RegNetX's group width, ResNeXt's cardinality-32
    blocks), and grouped convolutions carry much wider per-channel weight ranges than
    the dense convolutions in the seven interior models. No grouped-conv model in this
    set is CLE-free, and no CLE-on model here is group-free, so the two explanations
    are perfectly confounded in the sample available on this machine.
  - densenet121 is the informative near-miss: it touches the LOW edge 18 times and the
    high edge never. One-sided saturation is a different signature, and densenet121 is
    not a known accuracy casualty. So "touches an edge" alone predicts nothing; only
    the two-sided case tracks the failures, on two models.

The deciding experiment is a controlled A/B on one graph, in the shape of
scripts/quant-cle-probe.sh: quantize regnetx_002 through Ignition twice from the same
float export and the same calibration listing, once --cle and once --no-cle, and
re-measure saturation on both artifacts. If saturation disappears with CLE off, it is
equalization; if it survives, it is the architecture. Ignition emits both, so this
needs no vendor run.

NOT DONE HERE, and deliberately: that is a producer pass, and this machine is
Desktop 2 with peer sessions timing hardware. It belongs on Desktop 1. The exact
command is in the handoff entry.

